<p>Imagine trying to build a house by memorising the chemical formula of every single brick, nail, and beam before you start construction. Absurd, right? You would never finish. Yet this is exactly how most language learners approach vocabulary &mdash; word by word, definition by definition, as if language were a dictionary to be ingested one entry at a time.</p>

<p>There is a better way. It is called the <strong>lexical approach</strong>, and it flips the traditional grammar-first, vocabulary-second model on its head. Instead of learning individual words and then trying to assemble them into sentences, the lexical approach teaches you <strong>chunks</strong> &mdash; the multi-word units that native speakers actually use when they speak. The evidence is compelling: according to research in corpus linguistics, anywhere from <strong>55% to 80%</strong> of native speech is composed of prefabricated lexical chunks, not original combinations of individual words.</p>

<p>In this guide, we will explore what the lexical approach is, why it works so much better than traditional methods, and how you can use flashcard tools like FluentCards to build a chunk-based study system that will dramatically accelerate your fluency.</p>

<hr />

<h2>What Is the Lexical Approach?</h2>

<p>The lexical approach was formally articulated by <strong>Michael Lewis</strong> in his 1993 book <em>The Lexical Approach</em>. Lewis argued that language is not composed of grammatical rules with vocabulary plugged in as an afterthought. Instead, language is fundamentally <strong>lexical</strong> &mdash; made of multi-word units that carry both meaning and grammatical structure together.</p>

<p>Lewis identified four main types of lexical chunks:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Collocations</strong> &mdash; words that naturally go together: <em>heavy rain</em>, <em>make a decision</em>, <em>strong coffee</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Fixed expressions</strong> &mdash; completely frozen phrases: <em>How are you?</em>, <em>Nice to meet you</em>, <em>It's not my thing</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Semi-fixed expressions</strong> &mdash; frames with slots: <em>Could you please ...?</em>, <em>I'd like to ...</em>, <em>What do you think about ...?</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Sentence frames and heads</strong> &mdash; discourse markers and connectors: <em>First of all</em>, <em>Having said that</em>, <em>The point is that</em>.</li>
</ol>

<p>The key insight is that native speakers do not compose most of their speech from scratch. They retrieve ready-made chunks from memory and stitch them together with a small amount of novel construction. Learning a language, then, is largely a process of acquiring these thousands of ready-made chunks.</p>

<h3>Why Chunks Matter More Than You Think</h3>

<p>Consider this sentence: "I'm going to make a decision about that tomorrow." A traditional grammar-first approach would teach you the word <em>make</em>, the word <em>decision</em>, the future tense construction <em>going to</em>, the preposition <em>about</em>, and the pronoun <em>that</em> &mdash; five separate items to process and assemble in real time. A lexical approach teaches you the chunk <em>make a decision</em> as a single unit. You learn it once, store it as one piece, and retrieve it instantly when needed. This is not just more efficient. It is how native speakers actually process their own language.</p>

<blockquote>Language consists of grammaticalised lexis, not lexicalised grammar. &mdash; Michael Lewis, The Lexical Approach (1993)</blockquote>

<p>Research by <strong>Norbert Schmitt</strong> and colleagues at the University of Nottingham demonstrated that the human brain stores and processes lexical chunks as individual wholes. Because short-term memory is severely limited &mdash; the famous "7 ± 2" items at a time &mdash; retrieving one chunk instead of five individual words frees up cognitive capacity. You can think about what you want to say instead of how to say it.</p>

<hr />

<h2>The Science: What the Data Says</h2>

<p>The claim that 55&ndash;80% of native speech is prefabricated is not a hand-wave. It comes from systematic corpus analysis. Let us look at the data.</p>

<h3>Corpus Linguistics: Counting the Chunks</h3>

<p>In the 1990s, researchers at the University of Birmingham led by <strong>John Sinclair</strong> analysed the COBUILD corpus, a massive database of authentic English text. They found that about <strong>70%</strong> of spoken English consists of recurrent word combinations. A 2004 study by <strong>Alison Wray</strong> at Cardiff University, published in <em>Formulaic Language and the Lexicon</em>, refined this estimate, concluding that formulaic sequences account for between 55% and 80% of spoken discourse depending on genre and register.</p>

<p>These findings have been replicated across languages. <strong>Anna Siyanova-Chanturia</strong> and colleagues at Victoria University of Wellington, using eye-tracking and electroencephalography (EEG), showed that native speakers process collocations like <em>strong coffee</em> significantly faster than non-collocations like <em>powerful coffee</em> &mdash; even when the two phrases are matched for frequency. The brain literally has a shortcut for chunks it has seen before.</p>

<h3>Fluency and Processing Speed</h3>

<p>A landmark 2011 study by <strong>Diana Pulido</strong> at the University of Illinois examined how lexical chunk knowledge affected reading speed in a second language. Learners who had been trained on collocations and formulaic sequences read passages <strong>34% faster</strong> than those who had learned individual words, with no loss in comprehension. The chunk-trained group also scored higher on subsequent recall tests.</p>

<p>The mechanism is straightforward: every chunk you learn reduces the number of decisions you need to make during real-time communication. Instead of choosing between <em>make</em>, <em>do</em>, <em>take</em>, <em>perform</em>, and <em>carry out</em> when you want to say "make a decision," you simply retrieve the pre-assembled unit. This is the difference between reading a sentence letter by letter and recognising whole words at a glance.</p>

<h3>Second Language Acquisition Research</h3>

<p>Perhaps the most practical evidence comes from classroom studies. <strong>Frank Boers</strong> at the University of Western Ontario conducted a meta-analysis in 2006 of 12 studies on formulaic sequence instruction. Across all studies, learners who received explicit chunk instruction outperformed control groups on measures of speaking fluency, perceived oral proficiency, and native-likeness of expression. The effect size was moderate to large, meaning this is not a marginal improvement but a meaningful one.</p>

<p>Boers noted something interesting: learners who knew more chunks were perceived as more fluent by native speaker judges, even when their grammatical accuracy was no better than the control group. In other words, using the right chunks makes you <em>sound</em> fluent, which in turn gives you the confidence to speak more, creating a virtuous cycle.</p>

<hr />

<h2>How to Apply the Lexical Approach with Flashcards</h2>

<p>The lexical approach is deeply compatible with spaced repetition flashcard systems like FluentCards. Here is how to build a chunk-based study system.</p>

<h3>1. Learn Collocations, Not Single Words</h3>

<p>When you encounter a new word, do not create a card that tests just that word in isolation. Instead, create a card that tests the word in its most common collocation. If you are learning the word <em>opportunity</em>, your front should read something like <strong><code>(take | seize | miss) + opportunity</code></strong> with the answer being the correct collocation and a sample sentence.</p>

<p>FluentCards supports card templates that make this easy. Create a card type called "Collocations" with fields for the collocation pattern, a sample sentence, and the translation. Review these alongside your regular vocabulary cards.</p>

<h3>2. Mine Sentences, Not Definitions</h3>

<p>The legendary polyglot <strong>Kato Lomb</strong>, who learned 16 languages, famously said she "never studied words in isolation." She read books, extracted sentences, and studied those sentences as whole units. This is the essence of the lexical approach in practice.</p>

<p>When you find a sentence containing useful chunks, add the entire sentence as your card front. The back should explain the chunk. For example:</p>

<p><strong>Front:</strong> <em>I'm looking forward to hearing from you.</em><br />
<strong>Back:</strong> <em>look forward to + [verb]-ing</em> = a fixed expression for expressing anticipation.</p>

<p>This way, you learn not just the phrase but its grammatical pattern. FluentCards lets you toggle furigana for Japanese and TTS pronunciation, making sentence mining particularly powerful.</p>

<h3>3. Build Chunk Libraries by Topic</h3>

<p>Group your chunks into thematic decks. Create a deck for "<strong>Restaurant Chunks</strong>" containing: <em>I'd like to order</em>, <em>Could I have the bill?</em>, <em>What do you recommend?</em>, <em>Is the tip included?</em>. Create another for "<strong>Workplace Chunks</strong>": <em>Let's circle back</em>, <em>I'll follow up</em>, <em>Can we schedule a meeting?</em>, <em>Just to clarify</em>.</p>

<p>The advantage of thematic chunk decks is that they mimic the way your brain naturally organises language. When you walk into a restaurant, your brain does not query its entire vocabulary database &mdash; it primes the restaurant-specific network. Thematic chunk decks do the same thing for learners.</p>

<h3>4. Use Gap-Fill Cards for Semi-Fixed Expressions</h3>

<p>Semi-fixed expressions are the workhorses of fluent speech. Create cards with blanks for the variable slot:</p>

<p><strong>Front:</strong> <em>Could you please ___ me the salt?</em><br />
<strong>Back:</strong> <em>Could you please <strong>pass</strong> me the salt?</em></p>

<p>Next card:<br />
<strong>Front:</strong> <em>Could you please ___ me a favour?</em><br />
<strong>Back:</strong> <em>Could you please <strong>do</strong> me a favour?</em></p>

<p>You are training your brain to recognise the frame <code>Could you please + [verb] + [object]</code> as a single retrievable pattern with a variable slot. This is exactly how native speakers produce these sentences.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

<p>Switching to the lexical approach requires a shift in mindset. Here are the most common pitfalls.</p>

<h3>Mistake 1: Chasing Too Many Chunks at Once</h3>

<p>The lexical approach does not mean memorising every collocation in the dictionary. Start with the most frequent 200&ndash;300 chunks in your target language. For English, the COBUILD corpus identifies about 700 "common patterns" that account for the majority of spoken discourse. Learn these first, then expand.</p>

<h3>Mistake 2: Neglecting Grammar Entirely</h3>

<p>The lexical approach does not eliminate grammar &mdash; it recontextualises it. When you learn the chunk <em>I've been [verb]-ing</em> (e.g., <em>I've been studying, I've been working</em>), you are implicitly learning the present perfect continuous construction. Explicit grammar study still has a role, especially for languages with complex morphology, but it should support your lexical learning, not replace it.</p>

<h3>Mistake 3: Using Unnatural Chunks</h3>

<p>Not every word combination is a useful chunk. <em>Red car</em> is not a chunk &mdash; it is a freely composed adjective-noun pair. <em>Red tape</em> (meaning bureaucracy) is a chunk because its meaning is not predictable from its parts. Prioritise chunks that are both frequent and non-compositional. These give you the most bang for your memorisation effort.</p>

<h3>Mistake 4: Studying Chunks in Isolation from Context</h3>

<p>Some learners make the error of studying chunk lists like vocabulary lists, divorced from any real context. This defeats the purpose of the lexical approach. Each chunk you study should come from a sentence or situation you encountered naturally. FluentCards allows you to add example sentences and audio to every card, making it easy to keep chunks anchored in meaningful context.</p>

