<h2>Case Studies: The Lexical Approach in Action</h2>

<h3>The FSI and Diplomatic Language Training</h3>

<p>The U.S. Foreign Service Institute, which trains diplomats in dozens of languages, has implicitly used the lexical approach for decades. FSI courses emphasise "survival phrases" and "functional expressions" in the early stages. A diplomat learning Arabic does not start with verb conjugation tables. They start with chunks: <em>As-salamu alaykum</em>, <em>Shukran jazilan</em>, <em>Ana ismi</em>, <em>Min fadlik</em>. Only after acquiring several hundred such chunks does the formal grammar instruction begin.</p>

<p>The FSI's data supports this: diplomats trained with a chunk-first approach achieve functional fluency in Category I languages (Spanish, French) in approximately 24 weeks, while those using traditional grammar-translation methods can take significantly longer.</p>

<h3>What Corpus Data Tells Us</h3>

<p><strong>Paul Nation</strong>, one of the most cited researchers in vocabulary acquisition, has demonstrated that the most efficient route to reading fluency is learning the most frequent 2,000&ndash;3,000 word families. But Nation's point is often misunderstood. He is not advocating for isolated word lists. His research shows that learning words in their most common collocational patterns produces 50% better retention than learning the same words in isolation. The frequency of <em>take</em> as a standalone word is less important than the frequency of <em>take a look</em>, <em>take care of</em>, <em>take place</em>, and <em>take advantage of</em>.</p>

<blockquote>Vocabulary learning is most effective when words are learned as part of a system of related words and collocations, not as isolated items. &mdash; Paul Nation, Learning Vocabulary in Another Language (2001)</blockquote>

