<h2>Getting Started Today</h2>

<p>Here is your action plan for implementing the lexical approach with FluentCards:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>This week:</strong> Identify 30&ndash;50 high-frequency chunks in your target language from a corpus-based resource (e.g., the Oxford Collocations Dictionary, or the COBUILD English Usage resource). Create a "Core Chunks" deck.</li>
  <li><strong>Daily:</strong> Add 3&ndash;5 new chunks from your reading or listening. Each chunk must include: the full phrase, a sample sentence, and (optionally) a recording of a native speaker saying it.</li>
  <li><strong>Weekly:</strong> Review your chunk deck using FluentCards' FSRS scheduler. Pay attention to chunks you consistently mark as "Hard" &mdash; these are the patterns your brain has not yet automated.</li>
  <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> Test yourself by writing a short paragraph or having a 5-minute conversation using only chunks from your deck. Record yourself and compare to a native speaker version.</li>
</ol>

<p>Within three months of consistent chunk-based study, most learners report a noticeable improvement in speaking fluency. They hesitate less. They sound more natural. And crucially, they enjoy the process more, because they are learning language in the form it actually takes &mdash; as living, breathing chunks of human communication.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Summary</h2>

<ul>
  <li>The <strong>lexical approach</strong> treats multi-word chunks &mdash; not individual words or abstract grammar rules &mdash; as the fundamental building blocks of language.</li>
  <li><strong>55&ndash;80%</strong> of native speech is composed of prefabricated chunks, not original word combinations.</li>
  <li>Learning chunks reduces cognitive load, speeds up processing, and makes you sound more fluent.</li>
  <li>Flashcard systems like FluentCards are ideal tools for chunk-based learning: use collocation cards, sentence mining, thematic chunk decks, and gap-fill templates.</li>
  <li>Start with 200&ndash;300 high-frequency chunks, prioritise non-compositional phrases, and always study chunks in context.</li>
</ul>

<p>Stop memorising dictionaries. Start learning the chunks that real people actually use. Your future fluent self will thank you.</p>

